3. TYPED COMPARISON, NOT STRING COMPARISON — PASS

Source of the raw values (internal/models/user_profile.go:70-78):
    type FileInfo struct {
        Name         string
        Path         string
        Size         int64      <- raw bytes
        Enabled      bool
        Transactions int
        MinDate      string
        MaxDate      string
    }

MinDate/MaxDate are produced at internal/services/dataloader/loader.go:702-708:
        if !minD.IsZero() { minDate = minD.Format("2006-01-02") }
        if !maxD.IsZero() { maxDate = maxD.Format("2006-01-02") }
so they are ISO YYYY-MM-DD, and the empty string is the genuine "no parsed
range" sentinel. Because MinDate is a `string`, the template guard
`data-mindate="{{if .MinDate}}{{.MinDate}}{{end}}"` (filemanager.html:1379) is
correctly falsy for no-range files. (Had MinDate been a time.Time, that `{{if}}`
would always be true — checked deliberately; it is not.)

Row attributes (filemanager.html:1376-1381) carry raw, not rendered, values:
    data-name="{{.Name}}" data-size="{{.Size}}" data-rows="{{.Transactions}}"
    data-mindate="..." data-maxdate="..."
    data-enabled="{{if .Enabled}}1{{else}}0{{end}}"

The rendered File cell at 1384 is `{{.Name}}` plus a separate span
`({{printf "%.0f" (div (toFloat .Size) 1024.0)}}K)` — this is where the "25K" /
"4K" text lives. The comparator never reads cell text; it reads
`a.getAttribute('data-' + column)` (filemanager.html:487-488). Likewise the Date
Range cell renders `{{.MinDate}} - {{.MaxDate}}` (1388) while the comparator
sorts `data-mindate`.

EXECUTABLE PROOF. I extracted `cmp()` verbatim (lines 486-510, unmodified) into
a throwaway node script outside the repo and drove it with stub rows. Output:

  size asc  : small.csv=4096 , big.csv=25600
  size desc : big.csv=25600 , small.csv=4096
  lexical would give: 25600 , 4096 <-- WRONG order for reference
  mindate asc : b:2024-02-28 , a:2024-11-03 , c:(none) , d:(none)
  mindate desc: c:(none) , d:(none) , a:2024-11-03 , b:2024-02-28
  rows asc  : 9 , 25 , 100  (lexical would be 100,25,9)
  enabled asc: off=0 , on=1
  name asc  : apple.csv , file2.csv , file10.csv , Zebra.csv

  - 4096 B (renders "4K") sorts BEFORE 25600 B (renders "25K") ascending. The
    reference line shows a plain string sort inverts this. Byte count wins.
  - Empty date ranges land LAST ascending and first descending, exactly as the
    brief requires ("put them last in ascending order"), via the Infinity
    sentinel at lines 500-505. Deterministic, not arbitrary.
  - Rows sorts 9 < 25 < 100 (integer), not 100 < 25 < 9 (lexical).
  - Dates compare as ISO strings, which is order-equivalent to date comparison
    for the fixed-width YYYY-MM-DD format the Go layer emits.

The scratch files were deleted; final `git status --porcelain` confirms no
stray artifacts (see item 7).

