4. THE HIGH-RISK ITEM — SORT SURVIVES HTMX SWAPS — PASS

This is the item that looks correct on a static load even when broken, so I
resolved it by locating every node in the DOM against the swap boundary rather
than by reading the worker's comment.

(a) WHAT THE SWAP DESTROYS. The swap container is
    filemanager.html:211-213
        <div id="file-list">
            {{template "filemanager-file-list" .}}
        </div>
    and `filemanager-file-list` (define at 1334) is the ENTIRE
    `<table id="file-manager-table">` — thead, the four header <button>s, and
    tbody. All three swap sources target it with innerHTML:
        line 12   upload form   hx-target="#file-list" hx-swap="innerHTML"
        line 1393 On checkbox   hx-target="#file-list" hx-swap="innerHTML"
        line 1397 delete button hx-target="#file-list" hx-swap="innerHTML"
    So every swap replaces the header buttons with fresh server nodes: row
    order is lost, aria-sort is lost, arrows revert to `hidden`, and every
    click listener dies. A re-apply is genuinely required.

(b) THE STATE LIVES OUTSIDE THE SWAPPED REGION. `state` is a closure variable
    at filemanager.html:480 inside the IIFE assigned to
    `window.FileManagerSort` (479-586). That IIFE is in the <script> spanning
    lines 474-597 — a SIBLING of `#file-list`, which closes at line 213. An
    innerHTML swap on #file-list replaces only that div's children, so the
    script node and its closure are never touched. State survives.

(c) THE LISTENER IS ON A NODE THAT IS NEVER SWAPPED.
        filemanager.html:594
        document.body.addEventListener('htmx:afterSettle', function() {
            if (window.FileManagerSort) window.FileManagerSort.reapply();
        });
    document.body is not inside #file-list, so it is never replaced.

(d) document.body IS NON-NULL AT REGISTRATION TIME — the specific trap. If this
    script ran in <head>, `document.body` would be null, the line would throw,
    and because the DOMContentLoaded registration at 588-590 comes FIRST, the
    initial page load would still sort correctly while every swap silently
    stopped re-applying. I verified it does not: base.html has <head> 5-63 and
    <body> 65-254, and `{{template "filemanager-content" .}}` is invoked at
    base.html:161 — inside <body>. The script at filemanager.html:594 is within
    that block, so the <body> start tag is already parsed and document.body is
    live. base.html:192/199/209/218 already register htmx handlers on
    document.body the same way, confirming the pattern works in this app.

(e) THE EVENT ACTUALLY REACHES BODY. From web/static/vendor/htmx.min.js:
        new CustomEvent(e,{bubbles:true,cancelable:true,composed:true,detail:t})
    (`grep -o "bubbles:[a-z!0-9]*" | sort -u` yields the single value
    `bubbles:true` — there is no non-bubbling event path in this build), and
    afterSettle is fired on the settled elements:
        se(l.elts,function(e){ ... he(e,"htmx:afterSettle",o.eventInfo)});
    The settled elements are the newly inserted table inside #file-list, a
    descendant of body, so the event bubbles to the body listener. The event
    name string matches the camelCase form htmx emits.

(f) THE RE-APPLY IS CORRECT AND COMPLETE. reapply() -> wireButtons()
    (567-580) re-queries `[data-sort-btn]` on the NEW table and re-binds; the
    `btn.dataset.sortBound === '1'` guard prevents double-binding on the same
    node but does not block fresh post-swap nodes, which arrive from the server
    without that attribute. Then `if (state.column) applySort();` (line 579)
    restores ordering, and applySort() calls renderIndicators() (529) which
    re-sets aria-sort and the arrow glyph on the new <th>s. So ordering, the
    listeners, aria-sort and the arrow are ALL restored after a swap — not just
    the ordering.

(g) NO CONFLICTS. `grep -n "afterSettle|afterSwap|file-list"` over
    filemanager.html shows exactly one afterSettle registration (line 594) and
    no other JS path that rewrites #file-list.

Conclusion: the re-apply exists, is bound to document.body (never swapped),
reads state held in a closure outside the swapped subtree, and fires on a
bubbling event that htmx demonstrably emits. Toggling a file's On checkbox
leaves the sort intact.

